          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em homotopy hypothesis
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypothesis"} hypothesis
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/assertion"} assertion
            that
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} equivalent
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              at their
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
          %p or rather the stronger statement that
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoids"} n-groupoids
              are equivalent to this localization of those topological spaces which are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-types"} homotopy n-types
              for all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+natural+numbers"} extended natural numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mover
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \bar{\mathbb{N}}
          %p and moreover
          %ul
            %li
              this equivalence is induced by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
              construction.
          %p
            What this actually means in detail depends on which definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            is being used and to which precise incarnation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            it is being compared to.
          %p
            There are some definitions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            for which the homotopy hypothesis is a proven
            = succeed "," do
              %em theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
          %p
            Depending on where in the spectrum between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definitions+of+higher+categories"} geometric definitions of higher categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definitions+of+higher+categories"} algebraic definitions of higher categories
            a given definition of
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            For instance there is some justification for
            %em defining
            an
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em be
            equivalently a topological space considered modulo weak homotopy equivalence (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
          %p
            A definition of
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"} CW-complexes
            obtained this way is at the heart of all traditional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            A genuine algebraic definition of
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+Kan+complex"} algebraic Kan complex
          %p
            However for other algebraic definitions of
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Trimble+%E2%88%9E-category"} Trimble ∞-category
            stands out as an algebraic definition that has the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            built right into it, but also here it seems unclear at the moment how to make progress with proving the homotopy hypothesis.
          %p
            In fact, generally the homotopy hypothesis is regarded as a
            %em consistency condition
            for definitions in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %ul
            %li
              Any definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (n,r)-category
              should be such that there is a
              = succeed "-construction" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"} fundamental n-groupoid
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2C0%29-categories"} (n,0)-categories
              which makes these equivalent to
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy n-type
          %p
            One way to justify this condition is by recourse to the proven cases of the homotopy hypothesis: experience shows that the collections of all three models – topological spaces, Kan complexes, algebraic Kan complexes – provide a model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            that supports the general abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            – specifically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            – that one expects in analogy to how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            supports ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            theory. Any other definition of
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Apart from having different models of
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em equivalence
            between
            = succeed "-groupoids." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The usual, unstated, implication is that the notion of
            %em equivalence
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoid"} n-groupoid
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy n-type
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"}> n-category
              \-theoretic
            notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
          %p
            The reason this is important to specify is that there are other notions of equivalence on categorical structures which model homotopy types in other ways. For example, if we declare a functor between categories to be a weak equivalence iff its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            is a weak equivalence of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %em all
            homotopy types can be modeled by 1-categories in this way; see the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+structure"} Thomason model structure
            for 1-categories.
          %p
            Finally, in analogy to the homotopy hypothesis, there are also attempts to relate general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories"} (∞,n)-categories
            (not necessarily groupoidal) to
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+topological+space"} directed topological space
            = succeed "-construction." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} fundamental (∞,n)-category
            %em directed homotopy hypothesis
            can be proven, but at the moment there does not seem to be a published statement.
          %h2#AbstractStatement Abstract statement
          %p The following general abstract statement of the homotopy hypothesis is often useful to make explicit.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              There is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Π
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Pi \dashv |-|) :
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [W^{-1}]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              denotes the weak homotopy equivalences, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [W^{-1}]
              denotes the
              = succeed "-categorical" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            This statement can be formulated, holds true and is proven below at least for the standard definitions of these two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ForKanComplexes"} section on Kan complexes
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ForAlgebraicKanComplexes"} section on algebraic Kan complexes
          %h2#realizations Realizations
          %p
            We discuss various different definitions of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoid"} n-groupoid
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
          %h3#for_groupoids For groupoids
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
              of
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy 1-types
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              classes of homotopies.
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis+for+1-types"} homotopy hypothesis for 1-types
            for more.
          %h3#For2Groupoids For 2-groupoids
          %p
            (…)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy 2-type
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-groupoid"}> strict 2-groupoid
            s suffice (…) (but note that strict 2-functors are not sufficient to model all maps between 2-types)
          %h3#for_graygroupoids For Gray-groupoids
          %p
            It is known that not all homotopy 3-types can be modeled by strict 3-groupoids, but that
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-groupoid"} Gray-groupoid
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-strict+infinity-category"} semi-strict 3-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+product"} Whitehead product
            which arises from a nontrivial
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interchanger"} interchanger
          %h3#for_grothendieck_groupoids For Grothendieck ∞-groupoids
          %p
            For a review of progress on proving the homotopy hypothesis for Grothendieck’s original conception of ∞-groupoid in
            = succeed "," do
              %em Pursuing Stacks
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"} Georges Maltsiniotis
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a{:href => "#HenryLanari"} Henry and Lanari
          %h3#ForKanComplexes For Kan complexes
          %p
            We write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi sSet
                    %mi Quillen
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_{Quillen}
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            equipped with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi sSet
                    %mi Quillen
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_{Quillen}
            are precisely the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
          %p
            Also we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Top
                    %mi Quillen
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top_{Quillen}
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            equipped with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Top
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mi Sing
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Quillen
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (|-| \dashv Sing) : Top \stackrel{\overset{|-|}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{Sing}{\to}} sSet_{Quillen}
                    \\,,
            %p
              where the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing(-)
              is forming the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing(-)
            %p
              This induces an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
              of the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presented
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Quillen67"}> Quillen 67
            ), see e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#GoerssJardine96"}> Goerss-Jardine 96, section I.11
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#JoyalTierney05"} Joyal-Tierney 05, chapter I
          %h3#ForAlgebraicKanComplexes For algebraic Kan complexes
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+Kan+complex"} algebraic Kan complex
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+categories"} algebraic definition of higher groupoids
            obtained by taking the ordinary definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            and equipping these with
            %em choices
            of
            = succeed "-fillers." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
            %em pentagonators
            and so on.
          %p
            Algebraic Kan complexes constitute a genuine algebraic model in that they are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+over+a+monad"} algebras over a monad
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transferred+model+structure"} transferred
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+algebraic+fibrant+objects"} model structure on algebraic Kan complexes
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalent
              to the standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi Kan
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi F
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Quillen
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (F \dashv U) : Alg Kan \stackrel{\overset{F}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{U}{\to}}
                    sSet_{Quillen}
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The proof is spelled out at
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+algebraic+fibrant+objects"} model structure on algebraic fibrant objects
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              With the above
              %a{:href => "ForKanComplexes"} homotopy hypothesis-theorem for Kan complexes
              this gives a zig-zag of Quillen equivalences between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi Kan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg Kan
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi Kan
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi F
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Quillen
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo ←
                        %mi Sing
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Top
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Alg Kan
                    \\stackrel{\overset{F}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{U}{\to}}
                    sSet_{Quillen}
                    \\stackrel{\overset{|-|}{\to}}{\underset{Sing}{\leftarrow}}
                    Top
                    \\,.
            %p
              This already yields the homotopy hypothesis for algebraic Kan complexes at the level of the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presented (∞,1)-categories
              (as discussed there)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Alg
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Top
                      %mo ∘
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Alg C^\circ \simeq Top^\circ
                    \\,.
          %p But there is also a direct Quillen equivalence:
          .num_def
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^n_k
              for the topological
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
              and its
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
            %p
              Fix any choice of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    R(n,k) : \Delta^n \to \Lambda^n_k
            %p
              for all topological
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
              inclusions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mi i
                        %mi n
                      %mo ↪
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^n_i \hookrightarrow \Delta^n
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              equip the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sing
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing X
              with the stucture of an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+Kan+complex"} algebraic Kan complex
              by taking the filler of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sing
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda_k[n] \to Sing X
              to be given by the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (|-| \dashv Sing)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunct"} adjunct
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi n
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo ,
                          %mi k
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mi k
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n \stackrel{R(n,k)}{\to} \Lambda^n_k \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi Kan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_\infty(X) \in Alg Kan
              for the resulting algebraic Kan complex.
            %p This construction constitutes a functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi C
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Pi_\infty(-) : Top \to Alg C
                    \\,,
            %p
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo =
                      %mi Sing
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \Pi_\infty = Sing
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The choices of fillers in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_\infty(X)
              may be thought of as explicit choice of reparameterizations of paths in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+algebraic+fibrant+objects"} model structure on algebraic fibrant objects
          .num_def
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given choices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(-,-)
              of horn retracts as above, define a functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                    %mo :
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi Kan
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    |-|_r : Alg Kan \to Top
            %p
              called
              %strong reduced geometric realization
              by taking it on an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi Kan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in Alg Kan
              to be given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mi k
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi U
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    |A|_r := \lim_{\to}(\coprod_{\Lambda[n]_k \to A} \Delta^n \stackrel{\to}{\to} |U A|)
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi U
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |U A|
              is the ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
              of the underlying simplicial set of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and where the two maps are
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the image under
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|
                  of the distinguished fillers
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n] \to U A
                  of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %li
                %p
                  the composite
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi n
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mi k
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Λ
                          %mi k
                          %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi U
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n \stackrel{R(n,k)}{\to} \Lambda^n_k \to |U X|
                  \.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|_r
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_\infty
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (|-|_r \dashv \Pi_\infty)
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Nikolaus"}> Nikolaus, prop. 3.4
            ).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              We check the hom-isomorphism. A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : |A|_r \to X
              is by definition of the coequalizer the same as a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f : |A| \to X
              such that for each horn
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h : \Lambda[n]_k \to A
              with distinguished filler
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo :
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat h : \Delta[n] \to A
              the composites
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi U
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mover
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta^n \stackrel{R(n,k)}{\to} \Lambda^n_k
                    \\stackrel{|h|}{\to} |U A|
                    \\stackrel{\tilde f}{\to}
                    X
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mover
                          %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi U
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mover
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta^n \stackrel{|\hat h|}{\to} |U A| \stackrel{\tilde f}{\to} X
            %p
              are equal. This means equivalently that the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (|-| \dashv Sing)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunct"} adjunct
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sing
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde \tilde f : U A \to Sing X
              sends distinguished fillers in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              to distinguished fillers in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_\infty(X)
              and is hence a morphism in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi Kan
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg Kan
            %p
              This construction shows that the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f : |A|_r \to X) \mapsto (\tilde \tilde f : A \to \Pi_\infty(X))
              thus obtained is a bijection.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p We have an identity:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Alg
                            %mi Kan
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi U
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↖
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Π
                                    %mn ∞
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi sSet
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ←
                              %mi Sing
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; Alg Kan
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{U}}\swarrow
                    \&amp; \Downarrow^{=}&amp;
                    \\nwarrow^{\mathrlap{\Pi_\infty}}
                    \\\
                    sSet &amp;&amp;\underset{Sing}{\leftarrow}&amp;&amp; Top
                    }
            %p
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Alg
                            %mi Kan
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi F
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                  %mo −
                                  %msub
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                    %mi r
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi sSet
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                %mo −
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Top
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; Alg Kan
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{F}}\nearrow
                    \&amp; \Downarrow^{\simeq}&amp;
                    \\searrow^{\mathrlap{|-|_r}}
                    \\\
                    sSet &amp;&amp;\underset{|-|}{\to}&amp;&amp; Top
                    }
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Nikolaus"}> Nikolaus, corollary 3.5
            )
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              The identity is evident by definition of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_\infty
            %p Using this, we have that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo =
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (|-|_r \circ F \dashv U \circ \Pi_\infty = Sing)
                    \\,.
            %p
              So
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|_r \circ F
              is another
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sing
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing
              and hence naturally isomorphism to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|
          .num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p The adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi r
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (|-|_r \dashv \Pi_\infty)
                    \:
                    Alg C \to Top
            %p
              constitutes a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
          %p
            This is
            %a{:href => "#Nilkolaus"} Nikolaus, corollary 3.6
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              By the above theorem and the
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence#TwoOutOfThree"} 2-out-of-3-property
              of Quillen equivalences.
          %h3#Cubical For cubical sets
          %p
            Also
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
          %p
            There is an evident
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo →
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Box \to sSet
          %p
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube+category"} cube category
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{1}^n \mapsto (\Delta[1])^{\times n}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Similarly there is a canonical
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Box \to Top
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                    %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{1}^n \mapsto (\Delta^1_{Top})^n
                  \\,.
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve+and+realization"} nerve and realization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Top
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Sing
                          %mo □
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo □
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (|-| \dashv Sing_\Box) : Top \stackrel{\overset{|-|}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{Sing_\Box}{\to}} Set^{\Box^{op}}
          %p
            is the cubical analogue of the simplicial nerve and realization discussed
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ForKanComplexes"} above
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_5 Theorem
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+sets"} model structure on cubical sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo □
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\Box^{op}}
              whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  weak equivalences are the morphisms that become weak equivalences under geometric realization
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|
              %li
                %p
                  cofibrations are the
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          %p
            This is
            %a{:href => "#Jardine"} Jardine, sections 3.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_6 Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit of the adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo □
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A \to Sing_\Box(|A|)
            %p
              is a weak equivalence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\Box}
              for every cubical set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %p The counit of the adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo □
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    |Sing_\Box X| \to X
            %p
              is a weak equivalence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
              for every topological space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              It follows that we have an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
              induced on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories"} homotopy categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Top
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo □
                          %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Ho(Top) \simeq Ho(Set^{\Box^{op}})
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Jardine"} Jardine, theorem 29, corollary 30
          %h3#for__groups_and_fold_groupoids
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi cat
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^n
            groups and
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
          %p
            Loday’s notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cat-n-group"} cat-n-group
            corresponds to the connected version of an
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_7 Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cat-n-group"} cat-n-group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected"} connected
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy n+1-type
          %p
            This is proven in (
            %a{:href => "#Loday"}> Loday
            ). (There are some glitches in his proof and these were fixed by various authors (Steiner, Gilbert, ..) and then detailed proofs were given by Bullejos, Cegarra, Duskin and separately, using the equivalent formulation of crossed n-cubes, by Porter. Detailed references and some more commentary is at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cat-n-group"} cat-n-group
          %h3#for_segalgroupoids For Segal-groupoids
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              There is realization/singular complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi sSing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi SegalGrpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (|-| \dashv sSing) : Top \to SegalGrpd
            %p
              for
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+groupoid"} Segal groupoid
            %p
              Its unit is an equivalence of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+categories"} Segal categories
              and its counit a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
              of topological spaces.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              This is lemma 6.3.21 and corollary 6.3.24 in (
              %a{:href => "#Pellissier"}> Pellissier
              )
          %h3#ForStrictOmegaCategoriesWithWeakInverses
            For strict
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-groupoids"} strict omega-groupoids
            in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-categories"} strict omega-categories
            with
            %em strict
            inverses are far from modelling all homotopy types, strict
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %em weak
            inverses come closer. In (
            %a{:href => "#KapranovVoevodsky"}> Kapranov-Voevodsky
            ) it was argued that these are in fact sufficient, but a mistake in the argument was found in (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson"}> Simpson, Cor. 5.2
            ) (see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Simpson%27s+conjecture#History"} here
          %p
            The issue however is somewhat subtle, as highlighted by Voevodsky
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory#VoevodskyIASTalk2014"} here
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simpson%27s+conjecture"} Simpson's conjecture
          %h3#ForDgmSet
            For diagrammatic
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, 0)
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrammatic+set"} Diagrammatic sets
            are a topologically sound alternative to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computad"} polygraphs
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom+category"} atom category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shape+for+higher+structures"} geometric shape for higher structures
          %p
            There, an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, \infty)
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cell," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n + 1)
            = succeed "-cell." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \le n \le \infty
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, n)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, \infty)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt n
            are “weakly invertible”.
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              There is a model structure on diagrammatic sets whose fibrant objects are exactly the
              = succeed "-categories" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, 0)
          %br/
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              This is
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#ChanavatHadzihasanovic2024Model"} Corollary 5.8
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %h3#for_stratified_spaces For stratified spaces
          %p
            For a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorified"} categorified
            version which finds an equivalence for
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, 1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+homotopy+hypothesis"} stratified homotopy hypothesis
            (
            %a{:href => "#AFR15"}> Ayala-Francis-Rozenblyum
            ).
          %h3#for_spectra For spectra
          %p
            For a stabilized version which finds an equivalence between stable homotopy types (i.e.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            = succeed "-groupoids," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+hypothesis"} stable homotopy hypothesis
            (
            %a{:href => "#JO12"}> Johnson-Osorno
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#GJO17"} Gurski-Johnson-Osorno
          %h2#References References and a bit of history.
          %p
            The equivalence between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical homotopy theory of topological spaces
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} homotopy theory of Kan complexes
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Quillen67
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"}> Daniel Quillen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homotopical Algebra
          %p Later in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
              = succeed "\u2019" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} Daniel Quillen
          %p
            it was argued that there were
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definitions+of+higher+categories"} algebraic definitions of higher groupoids
            that could be put forward, so that the resulting objects ought to have a homotopy theory equivalent to the classical homotopy theory of topological spaces, and, moreover, would provide useful tools in the interpretation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            classes. This extended ideas that Grothendieck had explored in letters to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Larry+Breen"} Larry Breen
            in the mid 1970s in which he had given a sketch of a theory of
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-type" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            At this stage, (in the 1970s and early 1980s) more
            %em geometric
            or
            %em combinatorial
            definitions of infinity categories were not yet available, or, perhaps more accurately, had been discovered, but were not
            %em recognised
            as having such an infinity categoric interpretation; see
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim Porter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Spaces+as+infinity-groupoids.pdf", :title => ""} here
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/New+Spaces+for+Mathematics+and+Physics"} New Spaces for Mathematics and Physics
            = succeed "es," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategory"} weak Kan complex
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p The name “Homotopy Hypothesis” for this statement is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Homotopy Hypothesis
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/homotopy/"} web
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/homotopy/homotopy.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            More recently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"} Georges Maltsiniotis
            has revived the approach to
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em Pursuing Stacks
            \- see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} Grothendieck ∞-groupoids
            \- and work has begun on proving the homotopy hypothesis for Grothendieck ∞-groupoids. For a review of progress so far, see:
          %ul
            %li#HenryLanari
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Henry"}> Simon Henry
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edoardo+Lanari"} Edoardo Lanari
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the homotopy hypothesis in dimension 3
              %strong 39
              26 (2023) 735-768 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.05625"}> arxiv/1905.05625
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/39/26/39-26abs.html"} tac:39-26
              = succeed "&rbrack;." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/39/26/39-26.pdf"} pdf
          %p These authors have reduced the hypothesis to a conjecture which has so far been proved only up to dimension 3, hence the title of their paper.
          %p Technical reviews of Quillen’s proof of a version of the homotopy hypothesis include:
          %ul
            %li#GoerssJardine96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Goerss"}> Paul Goerss
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rick+Jardine"} Rick Jardine
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simplicial+homotopy+theory"} Simplicial homotopy theory
            %li#JoyalTierney05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Myles+Tierney"} Myles Tierney
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An introduction to simplicial homotopy theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://hopf.math.purdue.edu/cgi-bin/generate?/Joyal-Tierney/JT-chap-01"} chapter I
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://mat.uab.cat/~kock/crm/hocat/advanced-course/Quadern47.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The homotopy hypothesis for strict
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %ul
            %li#KapranovVoevodsky
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Kapranov"}> Mikhail Kapranov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  \-groupoids and homotopy types
          %p but a mistake in the argument was pointed out in Corollary 5.2 of
          %ul
            %li#Simpson
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
              ,
              %em Homotopy types of strict 3-groupoids
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9810059"}> arXiv:math/9810059
              )
          %p
            The homotopy hypothesis for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+Kan+complexes"} algebraic Kan complexes
            is established and discussed in
          %ul#Nikolaus
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Nikolaus"}> Thomas Nikolaus
              ,
              %em Algebraic models for higher categories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1003.1342"}> arXiv
              )
          %p For progress on
          %p
            The homotopy hypothesis for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+groupoids"} Segal groupoids
            is formulated in section 6.3.4 of
          %ul
            %li
              Regis Pellissier,
              %em Weak enriched categories - Categories enrichies faibles
              PhD Thesis (2002) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0308246"}> arXiv:math/0308246
              )
          %p
            Models of homotopy
            = succeed "-types" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-groups" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat^n
          %ul#Loday
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Loday"}> Jean-Louis Loday
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Spaces with finitely many non-trivial homotopy groups
          %p More literature on models of homotopy types by strict higher groupoids is at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
                ,
                %em
                  Computing Homotopy Types Using Crossed
                  = succeed "-Cubes" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi N
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0109091"}> arXiv
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simona+Paoli"}> Simona Paoli
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Internal categorical structures in homotopical algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/johnbaez/show/Towards+Higher+Categories"} Towards Higher Categories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.mq.edu.au/~simonap/paoli_IMA.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p The first paper, as its title suggests, has an emphasis on using higher groupoids for computation of homotopical invariants, in fact by applying higher homotopy van Kampen Theorems. These theorems lead to algebraic colimit arguments in algebraic topology, implying results, often nonabelian, not obtainable by other methods. It is also remarkable that the precision of these results requires the use of strict structures, whereas the current emphasis in higher category theory is on non strict structures.
          %p
            The homotopy theory of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
          %ul#Jardine
            %li
              Jardine,
              %em Model structure on cubical sets
              (
              %a{:href => "http://hopf.math.purdue.edu/Jardine/cubical2.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %ul
            %li Maltsiniotis, G. La catégorie cubique avec connexions est une catégorie test stricte. Homology, Homotopy Appl. 11, (2) (2009) 309–326.
          %p Cubical methods are also essential in
          %ul
            %li
              R. Brown, P.J. Higgins, R. Sivera,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Nonabelian algebraic topology: filtered spaces, crossed complexes, cubical homotopy groupoids
          %p
            Diagrammatic
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, 0)
          %ul
            %li#ChanavatHadzihasanovic2024Homotopy
              %p
                Chanavat, Hadzihasanovic,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Diagrammatic sets as a model of homotopy types
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.06285v1"} arXiv:2407.06285
            %li#ChanavatHadzihasanovic2024Model
              %p
                Chanavat, Hadzihasanovic,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Model structures for diagrammatic
                    = succeed "-categories" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2410.19053"} arXiv:2410.19053
          %p
            A version for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+spaces"} stratified spaces
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#AFR15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ayala"}> David Ayala
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Francis"} John Francis
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Rozenblyum"} Nick Rozenblyum
              = succeed "," do
                %em A stratified homotopy hypothesis
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1502.01713"} arXiv
          %p
            A version for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#JO12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niles+Johnson"} Niles Johnson
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Angelica+Osorno"} Angelica Osorno
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modeling stable one-types
                %a{:href => "http://tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/26/20/26-20abs.html"} tac
            %li#GJO17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Gurski"}> Nick Gurski
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niles+Johnson"} Niles Johnson
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Angelica+Osorno"} Angelica Osorno
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The 2-dimensional stable homotopy hypothesis
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.07218"} arxiv
